{"kind":"request","text":"Typed definitions: a readability pass on the authoring surface, before the\nlibrary is cut. Follows the amendment filed today (spike 2, commit\nf77e454a on spike/typed-definitions-20261028).\n\nSource: Hugh, 28 October 2026. After the second spike he asked for the\nsurface's accessibility helpers to go into the implementation request,\nand for further iteration to optimize readability. This request is that\niteration. It is design work with a witness, not a package.\n\n1. What to do. Rewrite the five definitions the second spike wrote\n   (lending, the live Counter, grow room and site, the change-lane slice)\n   with the eight helpers of the amendment (literals, take, by/via\n   declared once, grants and reasons, inline rules, quantifiers over an\n   item, items by value, errors at the source) and any further helper the\n   rewrite shows is missing. Then read each result against its row in\n   docs/lanes-reference.md or its definition's own README, and change the\n   surface until a reader of one can read the other without the\n   vocabulary page open. The pinned bytes stay the witnesses: every\n   rewrite compiles to the same canonical data as before.\n\n2. What to measure, on the canonical data and the source, by script.\n   - Code lines and bytes per definition before and after, against the\n     table in spikes/typed-surface/REPORT.md.\n   - The count of leaf operands written longhand (constant, nothing,\n     part, el) per definition before and after.\n   - The count of names written as strings (grants, reasons, subjects,\n     item names in field types) before and after.\n   - The vocabulary: how many exported builders a reader must know to\n     read each definition, counted by import.\n   - Time to first error: for five seeded mistakes (a state the item does\n     not declare, a field the act does not declare, a slot of the wrong\n     subject, a grant no role holds, a reason never declared), whether\n     the mistake is refused in the editor, by compile, by\n     validateDefinition, or only by the judge.\n\n3. What to decide and record. Which helpers earn their place by the\n   numbers and by the side-by-side reading; which proposed helper was\n   rejected and why; the final vocabulary as a one-page table of the six\n   patterns with one example each, generated from the builders' doc\n   comments so the page cannot drift. That page is the first artifact\n   the authoring skill (request ea166033) teaches from.\n\n4. Constraints. Operands stay plain data; modules stay functions with\n   explicit dependencies; the kernel is untouched; no package is published\n   from this request. The two definitions whose pinned spelling is not\n   canonical (grow room, change lane) are compared in canonical spelling\n   until the kernel request from the amendment's item 3 lands.\n\nCloses by a design note under notes/ with the measurements and the\nvocabulary page, and the rewritten definitions as its witness directory,\nlanded on main; or by a planner supersede that names the item carrying the\nwork.","body":{"conditions":"closes by a design note under notes/ with the readability measurements and the generated vocabulary page, with the rewritten definitions as its witness directory, landed on main; or by a planner supersede naming the carrying item","target_head":"06e4d33fe17937610891f29b5d1513b9e224edea","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}